{"tool_name":"cptac","contents":["bioconda","oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"cptac","version":"1.5.13","home":"https://pypi.org/project/cptac/","documentation":"https://paynelab.github.io/cptac/","license":"Apache-2.0","summary":"Python packaging for CPTAC data","identifiers":["biotools:cptac","doi:10.1021/acs.jproteome.0c00919"],"license_file":"LICENSE.md","build":{"noarch":"python","number":0,"run_exports":[null],"script":"-m pip install . --no-deps -vvv"},"requirements":{"host":["pip","python >=3.6"],"run":["beautifulsoup4 >=4.7.1","flask >=1.1.0","gtfparse >=1.2.1","mygene >=3.2.2","numpy >=1.16.3","openpyxl >=2.6.0","packaging >=19.2","pandas >=1.2.0","python >=3.6","requests >=2.21.0","scipy >=1.2.1","statsmodels >=0.10.0","urllib3 >=1.24.2","xlrd >=2.0.1","pyranges","tqdm"]},"source_url":"https://pypi.io/packages/source/c/cptac/cptac-1.5.13.tar.gz","test":{"imports":["cptac"]},"recipe_maintainers":["old-rob"]},"biotools":{"id":"cptac","home":"https://paynelab.github.io/cptac/","license":"Apache-2.0","summary":"This software provides easy access to cancer data from the National Cancer Institute\u2019s CPTAC program, which characterizes and studies the proteogenomic landscape of tumors. We implement the software as a Python package called cptac, but you can seamlessly use it in an R environment with the help of the reticulate package (demonstrated in Tutorial 6). Our package is installed in one step with pip:.","addition_date":"2021-03-19T09:05:23Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:29:45.594955Z","tool_type":["Database portal"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:cptac","documentation":[{"type":["Installation instructions"],"url":"https://paynelab.github.io/cptac/#installation"},{"type":["User manual"],"url":"https://paynelab.github.io/cptac/#documentation"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Database search","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2421"},{"term":"Enrichment analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3501"},{"term":"Pathway analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3928"},{"term":"Pathway visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3926"}]}],"name":"CPTAC","owner":"Kigaard","publication":[{"doi":"10.1021/acs.jproteome.0c00919","metadata":{"abstract":"Comprehensive cancer data sets recently generated by the Clinical Proteomic Tumor Analysis Consortium (CPTAC) offer great potential for advancing our understanding of how to combat cancer. These data sets include DNA, RNA, protein, and clinical characterization for tumor and normal samples from large cohorts of many different cancer types. The raw data are publicly available at various Cancer Research Data Commons. However, widespread reuse of these data sets is also facilitated by easy access to the processed quantitative data tables. We have created a data application programming interface (API) to distribute these processed tables, implemented as a Python package called cptac. We implement it such that users who prefer to work in R can easily use our package for data access and then transfer the data into R for analysis. Our package distributes the finalized processed CPTAC data sets in a consistent, up-to-date format. This consistency makes it easy to integrate the data with common graphing, statistical, and machine-learning packages for advanced analysis. Additionally, consistent formatting across all cancer types promotes the investigation of pan-cancer trends. The data API structure of directly streaming data within a programming environment enhances the reproducibility. Finally, with the accompanying tutorials, this package provides a novel resource for cancer research education. View the software documentation at https://paynelab.github.io/cptac/. View the GitHub repository at https://github.com/PayneLab/cptac.","authors":[{"name":"Adams D.W."},{"name":"Boekweg H."},{"name":"Kimball B."},{"name":"Lindgren C.M."},{"name":"Payne S.H."},{"name":"Pugh S.L."}